n? Not mentioned but nasty remarks suggests poor crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate C: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas. Strengths: situational awareness, technical cognition, leadership. Weaknesses: attitude (show-off, not open), could affect crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate D: responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, hot temper. Strengths: problem solving, responsibility, concentration. Weaknesses: hot temper, know-it-all attitude (cooperation issues).\n\nWe need best overall fit. All have some concerns. Which candidate balances? Candidate B has strong reliability and stress handling, but interpersonal issues severe (nasty remarks). That likely disqualifies for crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate A has good decision speed and calm, but communication difficulty and egocentric, not willing to learn. Also problematic.\n\nCandidate C has strong situational awareness and leadership, but not open to new ideas and show-off, could affect crew.\n\nCandidate D has strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but hot temper and know-it-all attitude.\n\nWhich is least concerning? Maybe D: hot temper could be serious under stress, but he responds adequately to unexpected events, takes responsibility. Know-it-all may affect cooperation but maybe manageable. B's nasty remarks are more severe. C's not open to new ideas could be safety risk. A's communication difficulty and egocentric also serious.\n\nThus D might be best despite temper. Let's propose recommendation: Candidate D.\n\nWe need to reference discussion: none, but we can say \"Given the initial overview, I propose...\"\n\nProvide evidence: D concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously (strengths). Concern: hot temper, know-it-all (weakness). Compare to others: